---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Chauncy FAQ" description="Frequently asked questions about Chauncy's local-first privacy model, launch status, and document curation workflow.">

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container faq-container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
      <h1>Practical answers before you trust Chauncy with your archive.</h1>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Can I buy Chauncy right now?</summary>
          <p>Yes. The Windows launch license is available on the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>. Checkout is handled by Stripe, then redirects to the Chauncy download page.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What formats does Chauncy support?</summary>
          <p>PDF, email (including IMAP mailbox sync), Markdown, Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, plain text, HTML, web URLs via the clipper, typed notes, and voice notes. If you have documents in a format not listed, contact us — we're adding formats based on demand.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Does Chauncy require an internet connection?</summary>
          <p>No. All core features — search, document ingestion, automatic connections — run entirely on your machine. An internet connection is only needed when you use an AI feature (summarization, deeper analysis), because those requests go to the API provider you've configured.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Is Chauncy a web app?</summary>
          <p>No. Chauncy is a desktop application. It runs locally on your computer and does not send your archive to a Chauncy server. There is no Chauncy cloud account and no Chauncy document server.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do I need an AI API key to use Chauncy?</summary>
          <p>No. AI features are optional. Search, ingestion, and automatic document connections all work without an API key. The AI layer adds capabilities like natural language summarization when you want them.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Is Chauncy an autonomous AI agent?</summary>
          <p>No. Chauncy is a retrieval and curation tool. It does not browse the web for you, operate accounts, send email, make purchases, delete files, or run background action chains. It helps you find and inspect your own material.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What does user curation mean?</summary>
          <p>Chauncy can discover related material and propose useful connections, but you decide what matters. Your annotations, disagreements, confirmations, and selections turn search results into your own working knowledge.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which AI providers does Chauncy support?</summary>
          <p>OpenAI, Anthropic, Groq, and compatible custom endpoints. Chauncy can also use local LLMs through tools like Ollama or LM Studio when you point Chauncy at their local API endpoint.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I use Chauncy with Ollama or another local LLM?</summary>
          <p>Yes. If you run Ollama, LM Studio, or another compatible local model server, Chauncy can connect to that local endpoint. In that setup, AI requests are handled on your machine instead of being sent to a cloud AI provider.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Who is Chauncy for?</summary>
          <p>Researchers, writers, analysts, consultants, and anyone managing a large personal document collection who doesn't want to spend their time organizing it. If you've ever thought "I know I saved something about this somewhere," Chauncy is for you.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Who is Chauncy not for?</summary>
          <p>Chauncy is not designed for compliance-regulated environments. If you operate under HIPAA, attorney-client privilege requirements, federal grant data handling rules, or similar regulatory frameworks, Chauncy is not the right tool. We're not built for that use case and make no claims to support it.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Does Chauncy sync across devices?</summary>
          <p>Not currently. Chauncy is a single-machine application. Your document collection lives on the machine where you installed it.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Is macOS supported?</summary>
          <p>Not at launch. macOS support is in active development. Join the waitlist at <a href="/waitlist">chauncy.dev/waitlist</a> and we'll notify you when it's ready.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Where do my documents go when I add them?</summary>
          <p>Into a local database on your machine. The full text, metadata, and vector embeddings are stored there. Nothing leaves your machine unless you use an AI feature.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I move my Chauncy database to a different machine?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Chauncy stores everything in a database directory you can locate in Settings. Copy it to your new machine and you're done. Documentation for migration is included.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens if I want to stop using Chauncy?</summary>
          <p>Your documents are yours. You can export everything and Chauncy stores nothing externally to delete.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .faq-container {
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .faq-container h1 {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.6rem);
  }

  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  details {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  details[open] {
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  summary {
    font-weight: 600;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  summary::marker {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  details p {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
